  + - `format/highlight.rs` — syntax highlighting for code, JSON, YAML, TOML. Day 169 (#759): under `norm == "rust"` **only**, a `'` no longer unconditionally opens a string/char literal. Rust lifetimes (`&'a str`, `fn f<'a>(…)`, `'static`, `'_`) never close their tick, so from that character on the *rest of the line* rendered in string colour — every Rust source line containing a lifetime displayed wrong, anywhere yoyo prints highlighted Rust (file display, `/find`/`/grep` context, tool-output rendering). The discrimination is **not** a second implementation: `is_rust_char_literal` delegates to `commands_refactor::char_literal_len`, the same helper `significant_braces` uses, made `pub(crate)` with this consumer in the same diff — so the char-literal-vs-lifetime rule has one statement and one table test, not a third quote scanner drifting from the other two. That reuse also closed a gap the highlighter's own deleted copy had: `'\u{1F600}'` now highlights as a char literal instead of rendering unstyled. **Named classes covered** (this is not a claim to lex Rust): `'x'`, `'\n'`, `'\''`, `'\u{7d}'` char literals highlight as before; a `'` that opens no *closed* literal is emitted as plain text, so the lifetime tick is inert and the code after it keeps its normal colours. **Deliberately unchanged:** every non-Rust language — Python, JS, shell and the rest still treat `'…'` as a genuine string, byte-identical to before, because the guard is gated on the language. **Still open, same as the sibling scanner (#771 items 1–2):** this highlighter is **line-based with no cross-line state at all**, so a `/* … */` block comment spanning lines and a string literal spanning lines are both mis-coloured from the second line on — that is *more* limited than `significant_braces`, which at least carries a same-file `in_block_comment` flag; nested block comments are unhandled in both. Pinned at the **emission point**: tests call `highlight_code_line` and assert the string a caller receives (lifetime case, `'x'`/`'\n'` pass-through, and unchanged python/js/shell single-quoted strings).
